 a different  startup that you can give some advice  and Momentum's and guidance some help  with somehow if the portfolio companies  can work together then you're not just  bringing an investment opportunity to  them you're bringing a new partnership  that can already help someone they've  already put money into so you're  bringing more to the table okay once you  found these different VCS the next tip  is to reach out and contact them and  that's pretty easy they all have an  email or phone number that you can dial  into you can send them a message you can  find out who the VC is you can see who's  accepting the business plans and send it  off to them the most important thing the  VC is going to look at and this was me I  was on I was on the screening side of  things I was seeing all the deals that  were coming in and you had to get  through me before you got to talk to the  people who are going to raise the money  you have to have an executive summary  that makes sense  think about two pages it defines  everything in your business plan that  has to make sense it has to be written  in plain English what we had an industry  term we use was the grandmother test it  has to pass a grandmother test your  executive summary has to be something  that you could show your grandmother or  somebody who knows nothing about your  business and they could read it and  understand it so as to be in plain  English has to not be filled with jargon  and it has to just make plain common  sense and a lot of companies when they  write their executive summary even their  whole business plan it's so technical so  filled with jargon that when they read  it it makes sense because that's the  world you live in but remember VCS  they're generalists they're not  specialists on a specific product like  you are so you have to make it make  sense for them if they can understand it  they're gone so show other people your  executive summary and your business plan  make sure it makes sense that's the  first thing I look at okay if your  executive summary is not a winner it's  gone  delete trash can it's gone maybe not  even response if they like it they're  going to dig deeper the next thing going  to look at is your team  the agency has this guide done it before  is there any startup experience at all  here the term when the VC world is is  this the horse for the course and so you  want to build as much momentum as you  can here if you've never had an  experience building a startup try to get  people on your board try to create a  Board of Advisors not a Board of  Directors which has more legal  implications but an advisory team get  people who can lend their name to the  project even if  not actively involved in the business  show that you're not just a one-man show  or a couple man show get people who've  had some experience on your advisory  board and the Weiser board is a loose  term it's just really getting somebody  who will lend their name to the project  and say hey I believe in these guys and  I'm going to help them out the next  thing I look at is your financials first  do you have any revenue if you have  revenue you're already separated from  ninety nine percent of the people who  apply most people just come with an idea  if you can have something that has some  revenue you're way ahead of everybody  else and they're not expecting you to be  huge that's why you're coming to them  that's why you need money the easiest  money to raise though is expansion  capital you figured out how something  works and there's a demand for it and  now you're ready to blow it up and you  need money to help do it so get some  revenue get some momentum as much as you  can I know it's hard at the start and  it's not going to be glamorous to say  hey I have $10,000 in sales but that's  10,000 more than most people so you're  already ahead of the game and the more  money you have coming in the better it  is they're not expecting you to have it  all figured out but shows some momentum  and then they're gonna look at your  projections can you throw off enough of  a return that they can make money do you  have enough growth potential in your  business if if it's a great you know  profitable company idea but it's not  going to grow at least 30% a year return  for the investor then they're not going  to be interested and then they're going  to look at the rest of the plan so it  starts with the executive summary then  the management team then the financials  and then the rest of the plan very  detailed hopefully this is helping you  the next thing is if they like you  they're going to call you in for a  meeting you're going to come in for a  meeting they're going to just scope you  out you know you've written a great  thing on paper and now they want to see  who the person is who's presenting this  idea and they're going to challenge you  on your business plan and they're going  to challenge you on your financials and  you better have an answer for everything  every number in your financial statement  you have to have a reason for and I know  a lot of it you're you're picking out of  the air but you have to be able to back  it up where you're spending the money  to be clearly defined how much do you  need and where is it going - and why do  you need to spend this much money on  this initiative you have to have solid  answers to that  if you waver you're not going to get the  money they're not going to think you  have the confidence to build this thing  up so practice drill it be able to  explain every single number every line  in your executive summary in your  business plan you have an answer for  practice practice practice and that will  help you win the pitch if they like you  so that first meeting goes well the next  thing we're going to talk about is  evaluation now that should already  either be in your business plan or you  have a clearer sense of what it's going  to be because if they don't like your  valuation then they're not going to find  you before the waste any more time they  have to know how much it is that you  want and how much of the company they're  going to get for I know what your bottom  line number is you know what you are  willing to accept and what you are not  willing to accept what you're going to  walk away from if you can agree on that  all of that they like evaluation they  like your plan they like your meeting  you all of that's going really well the  next thing you're going to do is do  diligence and this is usually a couple  weeks building it they're going to dive  deeper into your business they're going  to learn more about your industry learn  about your competition they will ask for  more records and information for your  company if you have an office they're  going to want to see it you have a team  they're going to meet them they're going  to dive as deep as they can to get to  know your business if all that goes well  then they're going to think about what  are the holes that you need to make this  thing really take off so for example you  might not be the best CEO for this  business you know if you have some  software company maybe your best place  is going to be leading the tech team but  not actually growing it as the CEO that  might be the advice that comes back and  that might be a deal breaker are you  willing to step out of your role as CEO  for somebody who's actually built up a  software company before and take it to  the next level they're going to come up  with a list of suggestions to help you  grow your business this is how they see  it growing they're going to give you X  amount of dollars and you'd like to see  these changes made some will be now and  some of  in the future but this is their  direction you have to be on the same  same page  you have to believe in that same vision  with them if everything is good if you  agree they're going to give you a term  sheet they're going to say this is how  much money we're going to invest this is  evaluation and you're going to have a  limited amount of time very short to  make a decision yes or no because what  they don't want to happen is you shot  the deal so you get a term sheet from  one VC and then go to another one and  say hey this VC gave me this deal so you  can you do better so you have a really  small window to make a decision and then  from there you say yes the one that you  like and you go forward so that's kind  of a twosie of how to raise money from a  venture capitalist  the biggest challenge typically is right  up front because 99% of business plans  don't get pulled in for that first  meeting so really do a good job with  your executive summary with your  business plan  the management BIOS the team you've  built and the the financials and  hopefully some revenue momentum and now  give you your best chance of getting  that first meeting and then it's up to  you to seal the deal if you have any  more questions about it leave it in  below leave in the comments and happy to  walk you through it that's kind of a  high-level overview of the whole process  in a couple minutes for those are you  watching like the video thumbs up below  I'll do more for you and I want to hear  we have to think leave a comment  underneath let me know you have a  question leave it below make a video  response for you guys thank you so much  and I'll see you soon

